    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d55">

                <pb facs="0055" n="45" sameAs="frus:frus1949v09/pg45"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.01/6–2149: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in India</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Henderson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">New Delhi</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-06-21T11:00:00+05:30">June 21, 1949—11
                            a. m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 12:13 p. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>700. During conversation Bajpai yesterday I asked if he could not further clarify
                    GOI attitude China. I was not requesting commitments but would like have
                    statement intentions. He replied “GOI <pb facs="0056" n="46" xml:id="pg46"/>
                    does not intend act unilaterally re extension recognition Communist Government
                    China or re other aspects its relations that government. It intends keep in
                    touch other governments and see what they decide to do”. Following brief
                    discussion Panikkar, Indian Ambassador to China, Bajpai assured me all important
                    decisions re China made in Delhi.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Henderson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
